WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.550
Many programs train at the end:
build, migrate, then train.

2
00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:08.112
The program's observation is
that this is paid for twice.

3
00:00:08.112 --> 00:00:10.138
The first payment comes at cutover.

4
00:00:10.138 --> 00:00:13.437
Users who weren't taught the
new sign-in call the help desk.

5
00:00:13.437 --> 00:00:17.996
A help desk that wasn't taught the new
verification either refuses legitimate

6
00:00:17.996 --> 00:00:22.413
callers or resets accounts without the
checks meant to stop impersonation.

7
00:00:22.413 --> 00:00:26.143
Administrators ask for standing
roles until things settle,

8
00:00:26.143 --> 00:00:29.351
and exceptions granted
under pressure tend to stay.

9
00:00:29.351 --> 00:00:31.462
The second payment comes afterward.

10
00:00:31.462 --> 00:00:35.362
The workarounds have to be found
and removed, people retrained,

11
00:00:35.362 --> 00:00:38.587
and habits unlearned, which
is slower than learning.

12
00:00:38.587 --> 00:00:41.987
Federal rules already put
training before the change.

13
00:00:41.987 --> 00:00:45.196
OPM's rule requires awareness
material before access,

14
00:00:45.196 --> 00:00:50.187
and training whenever the system environment
or procedures change significantly.

15
00:00:50.187 --> 00:00:54.726
NIST's controls ask for training
when system changes require it,

16
00:00:54.726 --> 00:01:00.463
and OMB Circular A-130 requires role-based
training before access is authorized.

17
00:01:00.463 --> 00:01:05.110
So training last also puts the agency
behind requirements it already has.
